  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nUpper level ridging over the plains region that has been persistent \nover the past several days will work its way eastward into the Great \nLakes heading towards midweek. High pressure that had been building \ninto the southern Great Lakes from the north will finally trudge \neastward after keeping the region in northeasterly flow this \nweekend. This will allow flow to finally turn southeasterly by \nTuesday, and then as the cold front approaches from the west, \nsouthwesterly with surface dewpoint advection, all while 500mb \nheights increase. Though the weather will be dry prior to Wednesday, \nexpect a brief period of very warm/humid air in the region late \nTuesday into Wednesday prior to the arrival of convection/the cold \nfront as temperatures and dewpoints climb. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nWill see low level jet, shear, and MLCAPE values increasing \nahead of the cold front for Wednesday into Wednesday night \nleading to higher confidence of thunderstorms for the midweek \nperiod. One thing to watch will be the track of the main surface\nlow for this system as it currently looks to push through \nnorthern Ontario/James Bay/northern Quebec with the upper level \ntrough as well. With the main energy that far north, will need \nto watch how the models handle the placement of the aforementioned\nlow level jet. As it stands, the stronger winds should be further\nto the northeast towards upstate New York/New England. So at this\npoint, still have many of the finer details to sort out for this\nsystem, but for now, it looks like lines of storms ahead of the\ncold front should be expected with at least a potential for \nsevere weather for now.",
